  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nDuring the early evening hours tomorrow, there are low chances \n(less than 30 percent) for locations south and east of Albany to see \nlight rain showers as a surface low pressure system moves to our \nsouth and east and the surface high pressure system slides to the \nnortheast. Then, mostly dry conditions but overcast skies return for \nlocations south and east of Albany through Monday morning. For \nlocations north of Albany, a surface low pressure system to our \nnorth brings low to medium chances for light rain showers Sunday \nnight into the early morning hours Monday. Probability of \nprecipitation was increased from null to 15-45 percent based on\nlatest high resolution model guidances suggesting drizzle to \nlight rain showers during the overnight hours Sunday into Monday\nmorning. A strong cold front is forecasted to move through the \nday Monday from west to east. Behind the cold front, drier \nconditions are in store for Monday night. \n\nA blustery Monday late morning and afternoon is in store across \neastern New York and western New England. Winds can range between 20 \nand 35 mph through Monday afternoon as conditions are favored to \nbring these breezy conditions from aloft to the surface. Higher \nterrain locations of the southern Greens, Berkshires, and eastern \nCatskills could see winds gusting up to 40 mph as confidence is \nincreasing for these windy conditions during the early afternoon \nhours Monday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nWhile Monday night into Tuesday morning is going to be chilly \nwith lows in the upper 20s for high terrain locations and upper \n30s to low 40s for valley locations, light winds will be \npreventing any frost development in valley locations. Tuesday \nnight into Wednesday morning is still supported by the latest \nforecast to bring calm winds and clear skies for frost to \ndevelop in valley locations as low temperatures range in the low\nto upper 30s. As we get closer in time, Frost advisories could \nbe needed for the Hudson River valley if confidence increases \nand the forecast remains steady for cool and calm conditions. \nFor reference, the normal low temperature for the beginning of \nOctober in Albany is between 44 and 46 degrees. Low temperatures\ncould range between 5 to 10 degrees below normal for this time \nof year across eastern New York and western New England. At \nleast through Wednesday morning as we return to near normal low \ntemperatures for Wednesday night into Thursday morning that \ncontinues through the end of the work week.",
